{"kind":"propose","text":"MULTI-RESIDENT COORDINATION: DIRECTION PROPOSAL v3 (supersedes v2 279bb63fbfc510acc9b29fefbd5326f2e6d13f00)\n\nThis revision answers CHANGES REQUESTED report cc65266c654813446d2b57cd1fbd531b8de8a231 (#5801) on assignment 78963842 (P1 backlog item 30218c6e...85fe10f1, #2545). v2 is superseded by this record and should be read only as history. Every file:line below was re-checked against main 84de35f6c048e156937df10d380874cb7180ee86; the lifecycle map in v1 sections 2 and 3 still holds and is not restated.\n\nWhat codex accepted and this revision keeps unchanged: one exclusive resident per repository; a second live resident refuses rather than joins or waits; takeover only after a definitive negative probe; refusal on every ambiguity; binding before ownership is contested but never serving before ownership is held; the listener closed on any loss; withdrawal guarded so a departing process cannot delete its successor's claim; a bounded, credential-free, side-effect-free loopback identity endpoint returning genesis alone; the Git CAS path of the durable log untouched; a shared nexus or leader election rejected as unnecessary complexity; the generation field cut.\n\nWhat changes is the ownership primitive, for the third time and, I think, the last: it becomes a Git ref update with an expected old value.\n\n== 1. THE DEFECT IN v2, ACCEPTED ==\n\ncodex is right and the missing interleaving is fatal. rename(2) moves whatever inode currently sits at a pathname; it takes no expected old value. Taker A and taker B both read owner claim X and both probe it definitively dead. B pauses. A renames X away, links a fresh claim, and serves. B resumes and calls rename(owner, owner.dead.B), which now succeeds against A's LIVE claim, moves it away, and lets B acquire. Two residents serve. The same thing happens if an ordinary newcomer links at owner during B's pause. v2's section 4 assertion that the second rename must see ENOENT is false once the pathname has been repopulated.\n\nThe general fault, which v1 and v2 both committed in different disguises, is reasoning about the atomicity of a PATHNAME operation when what must be atomic is a transition on a VALUE. link(2), rename(2) and unlink(2) are all pathname operations. None of them can say \"only if what is there now is still the thing I observed\". That sentence is the whole requirement, and it is the definition of compare-and-swap.\n\n== 2. THE PRIMITIVE: A GIT REF UPDATE WITH AN EXPECTED OLD VALUE ==\n\ncodex's report names this as acceptable, and it is strictly better than the hard-link-keyed-by-observed-nonce shape the report sketches, so I take it rather than the sketch.\n\ngit update-ref \u003cref\u003e \u003cnew\u003e \u003cold\u003e is a compare-and-swap on a named value. Git implements the mutual exclusion itself, with its own ref lockfile, and it is the same primitive that already makes the durable log correct: the append at internal/kernel/kernel.go:392 is store.UpdateRef(ctx, Ref(genesis), commit, head), and the genesis creation at kernel.go:322 uses the create-if-absent form with an empty expected value. The wrapper already exists at internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:285 and needs no change. The ref namespace already has an application-level precedent for exactly this kind of per-repository pointer: internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:149, refs/gitseq/checkpoints/\u003cgenesis\u003e.\n\nThe claim is the content of a blob; the ref names that blob. Store.WriteBlob at internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:110 writes it, Store.Head at gitstore.go:290 reads the object id back, and the object id IS the claim's identity. This matters more than it looks: because the claim carries a fresh 16-byte random nonce, no two claims ever hash to the same object id, so an expected-old-value comparison is a comparison against the exact claim that was observed and probed, and the A-B-A problem that haunts value CAS cannot arise here. A pathname can be repopulated; an object id cannot be counterfeited.\n\nThree operations, three exclusivity guarantees, all Git's:\n\n  create   update-ref \u003cref\u003e \u003cnew\u003e \"\"            fails if the ref exists\n  take     update-ref \u003cref\u003e \u003cnew\u003e \u003cobserved\u003e    fails unless the ref is still exactly \u003cobserved\u003e\n  release  update-ref -d \u003cref\u003e \u003cmine\u003e           deletes only if the ref is still exactly mine\n\nMeasured, not assumed. Against git 2.50.1 on the supported host, with 40 concurrent processes racing each operation on one ref:\n\n  40 concurrent creates on an absent ref: exactly one succeeded; the other 39 got \"cannot lock ref: reference already exists\".\n  40 concurrent takes from one observed value X: exactly one succeeded; the other 39 got \"cannot lock ref: is at \u003cwinner\u003e but expected X\".\n  20 concurrent CAS-deletes with the same expected value: exactly one succeeded.\n  A take whose expected value names a ref that has since been deleted: refused, \"unable to resolve reference\".\n\nThe third and fourth results are the ones v2 could not produce with rename(2).\n\n== 3. THE PROTOCOL ==\n\nTwo records, one authority, as codex accepted. The authority is the ref refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e, whose blob is the claim: genesis, the bound URL, a fresh 16-byte random owner nonce, and the PID as a diagnostic only. The advertisement at MetaDir/resident.json is demoted to endpoint metadata written only by a process that already holds ownership; internal/app/resident.go:55-61 keeps its shape and meaning, so ResidentURL and every existing reader keep working unchanged.\n\nThe ref is per repository by construction, which is what the request's alias requirement asks for. MetaDir is commonDir/gitseq (internal/app/app.go:356) and the store is opened on commonDir (app.go:372), so every path alias, every symlink, and every linked worktree of one repository resolves through ResolveGitDirs to one common directory and therefore one ref store and one ref. refs/gitseq/resident/* is an ordinary shared ref, not one of Git's per-worktree namespaces. Two distinct repositories have distinct common directories and, additionally, distinct genesis names, so they never contend.\n\n  ACQUIRE:\n    blob := WriteBlob(claim)\n    UpdateRef(ref, blob, \"\")       -\u003e held; go to SERVE\n    failure                        -\u003e go to CONTEST\n\n  CONTEST (bounded retries, then REFUSE):\n    observed := Head(ref)\n    ref absent                     -\u003e ACQUIRE\n    blob unreadable, oversized, or malformed -\u003e REFUSE (never vacant)\n    otherwise                      -\u003e PROBE the claim's URL\n\n  PROBE(url), short deadline, GET /v0/identity:\n    answers, genesis equals the claim's   -\u003e alive             -\u003e REFUSE, naming the URL\n    answers, genesis differs              -\u003e ambiguous         -\u003e REFUSE\n    dial refused, nothing listening       -\u003e definitively dead -\u003e TAKE\n    timeout, connected but silent, unparseable, any other error -\u003e ambiguous -\u003e REFUSE\n\n  TAKE (only after a definitive negative):\n    blob := WriteBlob(our claim)\n    UpdateRef(ref, blob, observed) -\u003e held; go to SERVE\n    failure                        -\u003e the claim moved under us; restart CONTEST\n\n  SERVE: only now is the bound listener handed to the HTTP server, and resident.json written.\n\n  WITHDRAW: CAS delete of the ref against our own blob. Best effort, and it can only ever remove our own claim.\n\n  REFUSE at any point: close the bound listener before returning, exit non-zero, name the incumbent URL or the offending blob and the recovery step.\n\nBind order is unchanged from v2 and from today's code: the listener is bound first, because the claim must carry the real URL and only binding resolves port zero (cmd/gs/main.go:1686-1689). Ownership, not binding, authorizes serving.\n\nWithdrawal remains an optimization, not a correctness requirement. A claim left by a crash costs the next starter one refused dial. The invariant is that two processes never serve one repository, and a stale claim never violates it because no claim is ever trusted as live without a probe.\n\n== 4. INTERLEAVINGS, EACH WITH ONE WINNER ==\n\n1. Two simultaneous fresh starters. Both bind different ports, both write their claim blob, both create. Git admits exactly one. The loser contests, probes the winner, finds it alive, closes its listener and exits non-zero without serving.\n\n2. Two takers racing one dead claim X. Both probe X definitively dead, both take from X. Git admits exactly one. The loser's expected value no longer matches, so it restarts CONTEST, reads the winner's claim, probes it alive, and refuses.\n\n3. THE DELAYED SECOND TAKER, WHICH BROKE v2. B observes X and probes it dead, then pauses arbitrarily long. A takes X, serves, and may in the meantime have withdrawn and been replaced by newcomer C, or by D after C. B resumes and takes from X. Every case fails at the same comparison: the ref is A's blob, or C's, or D's, or absent, and none of those equals X. There is no repopulation that can make it equal X, because X's object id is the hash of a claim containing a random nonce that no other process generates and that A never regenerates on restart. B restarts CONTEST and either refuses to a live incumbent or acquires an ownerless ref honestly. This is the interleaving codex named, and it is closed by the primitive rather than by an argument about ordering.\n\n4. A taker racing the incumbent's clean shutdown. The incumbent closes its listener, then releases with its own expected value. If the taker's probe fell in the window and it takes first, the incumbent's release finds the ref no longer equal to its own claim and does nothing. If the release lands first, the taker's take finds the ref absent and refused, restarts CONTEST, and creates. Either order yields one winner, and the guard against a departing process deleting its successor is the expected old value itself, not a separate nonce check.\n\n5. A crash mid-acquisition. The process died between writing its blob and updating the ref. The blob is unreferenced and Git prunes it in the ordinary course; the ref is untouched, so the next starter sees exactly the state that existed before. There is no partial state to recognize and no leftover file to sweep.\n\n6. A crash mid-takeover. Identical to case 5: the CAS either happened or did not.\n\n== 5. WHY THIS RATHER THAN THE ALTERNATIVES ==\n\nAgainst codex's hard-link-keyed-by-observed-nonce sketch. That shape can be made correct, and I walked it before rejecting it. It needs a deterministic transfer name, an inode comparison after linking to detect that the pathname was repopulated, an unlink of the owner name that is STILL not conditional on the inode, a sweep for orphaned transfer links, and a specified recovery for a pre-existing transfer link left by a crashed taker - which cannot be distinguished from an in-flight one without a second liveness probe, at which point the design has grown a second probe protocol to protect the first. Every one of those states exists only to simulate an expected-old-value comparison that Git already performs. The ref CAS has no tombstones, no temporary files, no orphan states, no sweep, and no wedge, so the crash and pre-existing-tombstone recovery the report asks to be specified is answered by deleting the states rather than by handling them.\n\nAgainst an advisory lock (flock or POSIX record locking). Automatic release on process death is genuinely attractive, and it is still the wrong trade: it makes liveness a property of a held file descriptor rather than of a served room, so a wedged process that holds the descriptor and answers nothing reads as alive; it needs a platform-specific syscall the tree has nowhere today, with golang.org/x/sys only an indirect dependency; and its behaviour over network filesystems is worse than the alternatives, not better.\n\nAgainst v2's link(2) acquisition. Acquisition was sound and codex accepted it. It is dropped anyway, because keeping it would leave the codebase with two different exclusion primitives for one lock - a file link for the fresh case and something else for the transfer case - where one primitive covers both. That is the simplification the report asked for in a different place.\n\nThe honest cost of the ref CAS is two Git subprocesses at startup and one at shutdown, on a path that already runs many. The honest limitation is that the lock is exactly as strong as Git's ref locking on the underlying filesystem. That is a better position than v2's, and worth stating precisely rather than hedging: a filesystem on which Git's ref locking is unreliable is a filesystem on which the durable log itself is unreliable, because the log's append is the same CAS on the same ref store. The resident lock therefore adds no new assumption to the system. It inherits one the system already makes and cannot function without.\n\n== 6. PHASE-2 SHAPE ==\n\nBranch request/multi-resident-coordination, as the request records.\n\ninternal/app/resident.go gains the ownership claim: ACQUIRE, CONTEST, TAKE, WITHDRAW, the owner nonce, and the ref name; PublishResident keeps its name and meaning but becomes reachable only to a holder, and its PID-guarded withdrawal (resident.go:41-48) is replaced by the expected-old-value release. internal/gitstore/gitstore.go gains a CAS delete beside UpdateRef and a bounded blob read; both are small and sit beside their existing neighbours. cmd/gs/main.go:1654-1719 gains the ownership step between binding and serving, plus the refusal messages. internal/service/server.go gains GET /v0/identity returning genesis alone, beside the routes at server.go:92-116. internal/residentclient/client.go carries the probe, reusing ValidateURL at client.go:51-65 and a bounded read. docs/reference/architecture.md documents the ownership-versus-advertisement contract in the same head. docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md:108-119 and docs/reference/gs/serve.md:121-128 replace \"Nothing enforces it: there is no lock\" with what is now enforced, the ambiguous-probe refusal, and the recovery story.\n\nTests, each stated as the interleaving it pins: two simultaneous starters with exactly one serving; a bound loser proven never to have served; a malformed and a truncated claim each refused rather than treated as vacant; stale-owner recovery after a definitive negative; a hung listener that accepts but never answers refused rather than taken over; a port inherited by a different genesis refused; two takers racing one stale claim with exactly one winner; THE DELAYED SECOND TAKER of section 4 case 3, driven deterministically by holding the observed value while a second process completes a full takeover, which is the mutation-sensitive test the report requires; a taker racing a clean shutdown in both orders; graceful shutdown releasing the claim; path aliases, symlinks and a linked worktree contending for one claim while two distinct repositories serve concurrently; and the existing kernel concurrency tests passing unchanged to show the durable CAS path untouched. Wire parity and public surface gates cover the new endpoint.\n\nArtifacts at the exact live strings the merge changes: internal/app, internal/gitstore, cmd/gs, internal/service, internal/residentclient, and the docs paths live at merge time. Several of the live artifacts at those paths are codex's, and the fold admits a bare supersession only from the author or an actor holding ratifier, so codex or hugh must retire those at step 5.\n\n== 7. WHAT I AM ASKING THE REVIEWER TO DECIDE ==\n\nWhether a Git ref update with an expected old value is accepted as the single acquisition, takeover and release primitive; whether refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e is the right name and namespace beside refs/gitseq/checkpoints/\u003cgenesis\u003e; whether the six interleavings in section 4 are complete, and specifically whether the nonce-in-the-blob argument against A-B-A is accepted as sound; whether making the resident lock inherit the durable log's existing filesystem assumption, rather than adding one, is the right way to bound the network-filesystem limitation; and whether the phase-2 test list closes the defect the report identified.\n\nNo implementation has begun. Phase 2 starts after this direction is approved."}